From c8a208d86ea1a09358e00fa225ab8e5d07caf03f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 10:14:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: ipa: add stop_on_suspend to force GSI channel stop on
 IPA v3.x

IPA v3.1 as found on msm8998 hangs the SoC on system suspend.
__gsi_channel_stop() and __gsi_channel_start() return early when the IPA
version is below v4.0, on the grounds that GSI does not implement
suspend/resume there, so the GSI channels are simply left running.  Only
ENDP_SUSPEND on the two AP endpoints is applied; the microcontroller and
the modem channels are untouched.  Suspend then gates the IPA core clock
underneath initiators that are still live, the interconnect stalls, and the
result is a hard hang that the watchdog does not reliably recover.

A matched A/B on a Pixel 2 XL isolates it to this driver: with the ipa
module unloaded, s2idle suspends and resumes cleanly; with the same kernel,
the same cpuidle configuration and ipa loaded, it hangs.  Module removal
works because teardown reaches the same channel stop with the version test
out of the way.

Add a module parameter that suppresses both early returns, so that IPA
versions below v4.0 take the real CH_STOP/CH_START path.  It defaults to
off, which is byte-for-byte the existing behaviour, so one image can serve
both arms of the experiment rather than one flash per arm.

The parameter is read-only after load rather than writable.  A channel is
stopped by one callback and started again by its pair, so a value that
changes between the two leaves them mismatched.  Setting it while the
device is runtime suspended was measured to make the following resume issue
CH_START against a channel that had never been stopped, which the hardware
rejects:

  ipa 1e40000.ipa: channel 6 bad state 2 before start
  ipa 1e40000.ipa: error -22 resuming channel 6

State 2 is GSI_CHANNEL_STATE_STARTED.  Read-only removes that window at no
cost, since reloading the module still selects either arm without a
reflash.

Tested on a Pixel 2 XL (IPA v3.1): with the parameter set, three runtime
suspends issued CH_STOP and the following resumes issued CH_START with no
error, so GSI does implement the commands on this hardware.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/net/ipa/gsi.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ipa/gsi.c b/drivers/net/ipa/gsi.c
index 0cb7649b972e..dbdd4be49966 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ipa/gsi.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ipa/gsi.c
@@ -923,13 +923,35 @@ static void gsi_channel_program(struct gsi_channel *channel, bool doorbell)
 	/* All done! */
 }
 
+/* GSI channel stop and start are skipped at suspend and resume prior to IPA
+ * v4.0, because GSI does not implement suspend/resume before then.  On IPA
+ * v3.1 that leaves the channels running while system suspend gates the IPA
+ * core clock, so the hardware keeps issuing bus transactions after its clock
+ * is gone and the interconnect stalls, taking down the SoC.
+ *
+ * Set this parameter to make IPA versions below v4.0 take the real
+ * CH_STOP/CH_START path instead.  It defaults to off, which is the stock
+ * behaviour, so that both arms can be compared without reflashing.
+ *
+ * It is read-only after load on purpose.  A channel is stopped by one callback
+ * and started again by its pair, so changing this between the two leaves them
+ * mismatched: flipping it on while the device is runtime suspended makes the
+ * next resume issue CH_START against a channel that was never stopped, which
+ * fails with -EINVAL and "bad state 2 before start".  Select it with
+ * ipa.stop_on_suspend=1 or at modprobe time, where it cannot straddle a pair.
+ */
+static bool stop_on_suspend;
+module_param(stop_on_suspend, bool, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(stop_on_suspend,
+		 "Stop and start GSI channels across suspend on IPA below v4.0 (default off)");
+
 static int __gsi_channel_start(struct gsi_channel *channel, bool resume)
 {
 	struct gsi *gsi = channel->gsi;
 	int ret;
 
 	/* Prior to IPA v4.0 suspend/resume is not implemented by GSI */
-	if (resume && gsi->version < IPA_VERSION_4_0)
+	if (resume && gsi->version < IPA_VERSION_4_0 && !stop_on_suspend)
 		return 0;
 
 	mutex_lock(&gsi->mutex);
@@ -984,7 +1006,7 @@ static int __gsi_channel_stop(struct gsi_channel *channel, bool suspend)
 	gsi_channel_trans_quiesce(channel);
 
 	/* Prior to IPA v4.0 suspend/resume is not implemented by GSI */
-	if (suspend && gsi->version < IPA_VERSION_4_0)
+	if (suspend && gsi->version < IPA_VERSION_4_0 && !stop_on_suspend)
 		return 0;
 
 	mutex_lock(&gsi->mutex);
-- 
2.55.0

